    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1534">

                <pb facs="0986" n="870" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg870"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 312.51/58.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Vice Consul Silliman to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi><anchor n="142"
                        xml:id="d1534-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Consulate</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Saltillo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-07-17T19:00:00-06:41">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 17, 1914, 7 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your July 16, 5 p.m. General Natera, Governor of Zacatecas, at present in
                    Monterey. Will take the matter up with him personally to-morrow. There are
                    twelve French Christian authorities [sic] remaining here. They received rough
                    treatment when Saltillo was occupied but are safe and unmolested. Was assured by
                    local government this afternoon that subject to certain restrictions they will
                    receive full protection.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Silliman</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1534-f1" n="142" type="source" xml:id="d1534-f1-content">
                <p>Communicated to the French Chargé d’Affaires July 18, 1914.</p>
